  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "amputation_knife_section_1",
      "section_title": "Amputation knife",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An amputation knife is a large surgical knife with a long, sharp blade designed to divide soft tissues during an amputation. It is used to incise skin, subcutaneous tissue, fascia, and muscle, and to help create the soft-tissue flaps that cover the residual limb."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Amputation knives are traditionally associated with limb amputation, although modern operations may use different scalpels, long knives, electrosurgical devices, and other instruments according to the operative technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is an amputation knife, a surgical cutting instrument with a long, sharp blade used to divide soft tissues and fashion flaps during limb amputation. It is designed to make controlled incisions through the soft tissues surrounding the bone.”"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Labelling and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise appearance varies by design. In an OSCE station, identify the components visible on the instrument presented."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Label",
                    "Component",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "A",
                      "Handle",
                      "Provides grip and control"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Blade",
                      "Divides soft tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Cutting edge",
                      "Performs the incision"
                    ],
                    [
                      "D",
                      "Blade tip or point",
                      "Initiates or extends an incision"
                    ],
                    [
                      "E",
                      "Blade heel or base",
                      "The region where the blade joins the handle"
                    ],
                    [
                      "F",
                      "Spine or back of blade",
                      "The non-cutting edge of the blade"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Some amputation knives have a straight blade, while others have a curved blade. The tip, cutting edge, and blade profile should be labelled according to the specific design."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An amputation knife generally has the following properties:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blade: Long and sharp to allow controlled division of substantial lengths of soft tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Commonly surgical-grade stainless steel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Handle: Designed to provide a secure grip and control over the cutting movement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blade profile: May be straight, curved, or otherwise shaped for the intended technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cutting edge: Designed for effective incision of skin, fascia, and muscle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Size: Larger than many routine scalpels, with variations according to the procedure and anatomical site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reprocessing: Reusable models require validated cleaning and sterilization; disposable versions must be handled according to their instructions for use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A key design consideration is the ability to produce a sufficiently long, controlled incision without requiring repeated short cuts."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Straight amputation knife"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a relatively straight cutting edge and is used for controlled incisions through soft tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Curved amputation knife"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a curved blade that may facilitate tissue division and flap formation according to the surgical technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/amputation_knife_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Long-handled amputation knife"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides additional reach and is useful when a longer blade and handle are required for the operative site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Amputation knives can also be classified by blade length, width, curvature, handle configuration, and whether they are reusable or disposable."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The amputation knife works by sharp-force cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The surgeon positions the cutting edge at the intended incision site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A controlled movement is applied through the handle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The sharp edge concentrates force over a narrow area."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The blade divides the soft tissue along the intended incision line."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The incision is extended as necessary to create the planned operative flaps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The knife is designed to divide soft tissue; it is not intended to cut through thick bone. Bone division is performed with appropriate instruments, such as a bone saw."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The amputation knife may be used to:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incise skin and subcutaneous tissue during amputation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Divide fascia and muscle according to the operative plan."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Create anterior and posterior soft-tissue flaps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Help fashion the soft-tissue envelope around a residual limb."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Make long incisions when a large cutting surface is required."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_7",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which it is used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Procedure",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Below-knee amputation",
                      "Divide soft tissues and fashion the residual-limb flaps"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Above-knee amputation",
                      "Incise and divide soft tissues around the thigh"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Selected upper-limb amputations",
                      "Divide soft tissue and create appropriate flaps"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Revision amputation",
                      "Remove selected tissue and reshape the residual limb"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Selected emergency amputations",
                      "May be used as part of an appropriate surgical instrument set"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise instruments used depend on the level of amputation, the condition of the tissues, the planned flap design, and the surgeon's technique."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Long incision: Facilitates division of an extended length of soft tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Efficient cutting: A sharp blade can divide tissue with controlled movement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flap formation: Useful for creating soft-tissue flaps required to cover the residual limb."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reach: A long blade and handle can provide access across a wider operative field."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Relatively simple design: Has few moving parts and is straightforward to inspect."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A long, sharp blade can cause substantial injury if poorly controlled."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its size may make it less suitable for delicate or confined anatomical spaces."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A blunt blade may require excessive force and cause tissue trauma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect flap design may compromise tissue coverage and healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is not designed to divide thick bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Safe handling and secure instrument transfer are essential."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Haemorrhage",
                      "Incision may divide blood vessels"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Soft-tissue injury",
                      "Poorly controlled cutting may damage tissues beyond the intended plane"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nerve injury",
                      "Major nerves or nerve branches may be injured"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inadequate flap formation",
                      "Incorrect incision planning may impair residual-limb coverage"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Wound breakdown",
                      "Poor tissue viability or closure tension may compromise healing"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Possible following inadequate aseptic technique or compromised wound care"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Injury to staff",
                      "Accidental cuts may occur during handling or transfer"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Delayed wound healing",
                      "May result from poor perfusion, infection, or excessive tissue trauma"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_11",
              "group_title": "Prevention",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure the knife is sharp, intact, and appropriate for the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the intended incision and flap design before cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain control of the blade and protect adjacent structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use safe sharps-handling and instrument-transfer procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure appropriate haemostasis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Preserve viable tissue and avoid unnecessary trauma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Follow correct sterile technique and wound-management principles."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Precautions and contraindications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The amputation knife is selected according to the operative procedure rather than having a universal list of instrument-specific contraindications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not use a damaged or blunt blade."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not use it to divide bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain awareness of major nerves and vessels."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid uncontrolled sweeping movements."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure the planned incision accounts for tissue viability and the required residual-limb coverage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Follow safe sharps procedures during use, transfer, and disposal."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For reusable amputation knives:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Handle the blade carefully to avoid sharps injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clean the blade, handle, and any junctions according to the manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rinse and dry thoroughly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the blade for chips, corrosion, dullness, or deformation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the handle is secure and intact."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterilize using a validated method compatible with the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For disposable models, follow the manufacturer's single-use instructions and dispose of the blade in an approved sharps container."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is an amputation knife?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A long-bladed surgical knife used to divide soft tissues and help form flaps during amputation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is its main principle of operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sharp-force cutting, in which a sharp edge concentrates force to divide tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What tissues can it divide?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Skin, subcutaneous tissue, fascia, and muscle, depending on the operative technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Does it cut bone?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Bone is divided using an appropriate bone saw or another instrument designed for bone cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why is flap formation important in amputation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Appropriate viable soft-tissue flaps help cover the residual limb and support wound closure and healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Mention four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Haemorrhage, nerve injury, wound breakdown, and accidental sharps injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What is the difference between an amputation knife and a scalpel?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Both are cutting instruments, but an amputation knife typically has a longer blade intended for extensive soft-tissue incisions, whereas a scalpel is generally used for more localized, precise incisions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why must a blunt amputation knife be avoided?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A blunt blade may require excessive force, reducing control and increasing tissue trauma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What should be inspected before use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blade sharpness and integrity, handle security, and any damage or corrosion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. What is the main safety priority?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Controlled cutting, protection of adjacent structures, and safe handling of the sharp blade."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "amputation_knife_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "OSCE examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
