                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Explains sharp-force cutting",
                    "States the tissues it is used to divide",
                    "Explains its role in flap formation",
                    "Names appropriate amputation procedures",
                    "States advantages and disadvantages",
                    "Identifies complications and their prevention",
                    "Distinguishes it from a scalpel and bone saw",
                    "Describes safe handling, inspection and reprocessing"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "amputation_knife_group_16",
              "group_title": "Final high-yield summary",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identification: Long-bladed knife for soft-tissue division during amputation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main parts: Handle, blade, cutting edge, tip, and spine."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Principle: Sharp-force cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main uses: Soft-tissue division and flap formation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Major risks: Haemorrhage, nerve injury, wound complications, and sharps injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key distinction: An amputation knife cuts soft tissue; a bone saw divides bone."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
